\section{Let the Application Decide When to Load}

An editor might need an optional document filter only when a user opens a particular format. If the filter is an ordinary recorded dependency, a missing library can prevent the editor from starting. Levine contrasts such startup dependencies with explicit dynamic loading: a program can ask the runtime linker to load a shared object and then ask for particular symbols \cite[chapter~10]{levine2000linkers}. His comparison with older mainframe exit routines is useful: the idea of a packaged program admitting an extension is older than either ELF or POSIX. The mechanism described here is the ELF implementation on GNU/Linux, not an interface to historic exit routines or to PE DLLs.

Three events have to be kept separate. First, \texttt{dlopen} maps or acquires a reference to a shared object and processes its dependencies and relocations. Successful loading may execute initialization code before returning to the caller. Second, \texttt{dlsym} searches for a named symbol in the scope associated with a handle; that search can find a symbol in a dependency as well as in the named file. Third, the host decides whether to publish the resulting interface for use. An explicit load can still use eager binding: \texttt{RTLD\_NOW} resolves the object's required undefined references before a successful return, but it cannot verify that the object provides the particular entry point the host will later ask for.

\section{The Handle Owns the Right to Use the Interface}

Use a known, trusted plugin at a host-selected path and pass \texttt{RTLD\_LOCAL} with \texttt{RTLD\_NOW}. The local flag does not add its exports to the global scope used to resolve ordinary references in subsequently loaded objects. It does not create isolation: the plugin shares the host process and can run native code, and symbol resolution still interacts with the existing process. For a tiny synchronous interface the host and plugin agree that \texttt{scale\_v1} implements the C signature \verb|int scale_v1(int)|. On POSIX the result of a successful \texttt{dlsym} for a function can be converted to the correct function pointer type; a generic pointer and a matching string are not proof that the signature is correct.

A useful ownership rule is that a raw function or data pointer is borrowed from the live module handle. Copying the pointer does not acquire another loader reference. A real extension protocol should also specify the calling convention, representation of any structures, ownership of allocated objects, and how an interface version changes. An opaque host-owned wrapper can enforce the borrowing rule without exposing the raw pointer to its callers.

