\section{Closing a Reference Is Not an Unmap Guarantee}

On GNU/Linux, repeated successful opens can refer to the same loaded object. Closing one handle releases one reference; other handles, dependency references, or a policy such as \texttt{RTLD\_NODELETE} can keep the object mapped. Therefore a successful close does not demonstrate that the bytes have vanished, and a stale pointer that seems to work is not evidence of a valid contract. The host must stop exposing the interface after closing its reference regardless of what the loader does.

For a plugin with background work, count more than API calls. A pending callback can carry a plugin code pointer, and an object in host storage can require a destructor in the module. Define a shutdown protocol that stops new work, drains or cancels outstanding work, joins workers, and destroys plugin-owned objects while a handle is still held. Run the close outside the registry lock so library finalization cannot deadlock merely by trying to take that lock. Shutdown must not be invoked by an operation that holds a lease it is itself waiting to drain.
